.\" Manual page for the ls re-implementation of BSDSF24A025-OS-A02
.\" Programming Assignment 02 - Operating Systems
.TH LS 1 "13 October 2026" "BSDSF24A025-OS-A02" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
.RB [ \-lxR ]
.RI [ directory ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ls
lists the contents of each
.I directory
given on the command line, or of the current directory when no operand is
supplied.  Entries whose names begin with a dot are treated as hidden and are
skipped, exactly like the base behaviour of the real
.BR ls (1).
.PP
Every directory is read once with
.BR opendir (3)
and
.BR readdir (3)
into a dynamically allocated array of
.B struct file_info
elements, each holding the entry name together with the metadata returned by
.BR lstat (2).
The array is sorted alphabetically with
.BR qsort (3)
and rendered by one of the display functions described under
.BR OPTIONS .
The metadata of a symbolic link is never followed, so a link is described by
its own permissions, size and modification time.
.PP
When several directories are listed, each one is introduced by a header line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
Produce a long listing, one entry per line, preceded by a
.B total
line with the number of blocks used by the directory.  Each line shows, in
columns that are aligned across the whole listing: the file type and the
permission string, the number of hard links, the owner name (resolved with
.BR getpwuid (3)),
the group name (resolved with
.BR getgrgid (3)),
the size in bytes, the modification time (formatted with
.BR ctime (3))
and finally the name.  Symbolic links are additionally followed by an arrow
and the path of their target.  The special permission bits setuid, setgid and
sticky appear as
.BR s ", " S ", " t " or " T
in place of the corresponding execute character.
.TP
.B \-x
Print the entries in horizontal columns: left to right across the screen,
wrapping to the next line only when the current line is full.  This is the
.B across
layout, in contrast to the default
.B down then across
layout.
.TP
.B \-R
List recursively.  After a directory has been displayed, the program descends
into each of its subdirectories and prints a header line naming it, continuing
until the whole tree has been visited.  The full path of a child is built by
joining the name to the path of its parent.
.SH LAYOUT
Without any display option the entries are printed in columns that are filled
from top to bottom and then across, like the default output of the real
.BR ls (1).
The number of columns is derived from the width of the terminal, which is
queried with
.BR ioctl (2)
using the
.B TIOCGWINSZ
request; when the size cannot be detected (for example when the output is
redirected to a file or a pipe) a width of 80 columns is assumed.  The width of
a column is the length of the longest name plus two blanks.
.SH COLORS
Filenames are printed with an ANSI escape sequence chosen from the file type,
so that the different kinds of entries can be told apart at a glance:
.TP
.B blue
directories
.TP
.B green
executable files
.TP
.B red
archives (.tar, .gz, .zip)
.TP
.B pink
symbolic links
.TP
.B reverse video
special files (devices, sockets, fifos)
.PP
The escape sequences occupy no space on the screen, therefore the padding of
the columns is always computed from the length of the plain name.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ls
List the current directory in down-then-across columns.
.TP
.B ls \-l
List the current directory in the long format.
.TP
.B ls \-x
List the current directory in horizontal columns.
.TP
.B ls \-lR /etc
Recursively list the whole of
.I /etc
with full metadata for every entry.
.TP
.B ls src obj
List two directories, each introduced by its own header line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The listing was produced.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was given, or the program was invoked incorrectly.
.PP
A directory that cannot be opened is reported on the standard error output as
.B "Cannot open directory"
followed by its name; the remaining directories are still listed.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.nf
$ ls \-l src
total 16
-rw\-r--r--  1 daniyal  daniyal  7289 Oct 13 08:48 ls\-v1.0.0.c
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I src/ls\-v1.0.0.c
The single source file, extended by every feature of this assignment.
.TP
.I Makefile
Build script producing
.IR bin/ls .
.SH BUGS
Hidden entries cannot be displayed: there is no
.B \-a
option.  Sorting is done with
.BR strcmp (3),
so it is case sensitive and locale dependent, and entries whose metadata
cannot be read with
.BR lstat (2)
are still listed but shown with empty metadata.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR opendir (3),
.BR readdir (3),
.BR lstat (2),
.BR stat (2),
.BR getpwuid (3),
.BR getgrgid (3),
.BR ctime (3),
.BR qsort (3),
.BR ioctl (2)
